 {:db/id "q-105684" :question/id #uuid "84e2e7d1-ebc5-58ab-8dc8-1f209b929dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-105684" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\sqrt{x}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[1,4],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(1)$ and $f(4).$\n\nSince $f(1)=2$ and $f(4)=66,$ we must have \n$k\\in [2,66].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[2,66].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-105684/ma-source-field/eeb2438a6d8705502a3a42072684f614f995f6063924f7a9c63a3f28f1512701"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
