 {:db/id "q-106880" :question/id #uuid "3d0c58af-c68f-52d1-a3dd-2d55bdfa4769" :question/math-academy-id "q-106880" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{3}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 3$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{3}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3}(R))={3}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-106880/ma-source-field/261e2d0231aca4bcdd941284a0e9d0b192d8d759542e5028297ecbaef75bfbfa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
