 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "132107ab-2199-5d46-b51c-37977a6efa1d"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109390/ma-source-field/215e14deff61cd0a869a6ae6d2678e1918ea9cdc3d54a204c6870f26222d835c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
