 {:db/id "q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef" :answer-field/id #uuid "e03dd41f-134c-570f-ba29-59c8bf53a6bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :answer-field/correct "q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-109696" :question/id #uuid "854fff95-597d-57db-aef7-0e595418f814" :question/math-academy-id "q-109696" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-3{y}^{′}+4y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ a & c & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=3{y}^{′}-4y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $a=-4,b=1,c=3,$ and $a+b+c=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109696/ma-source-field/20dcc7fa31d8d2639d9169f0ebc13e24a0cb1b68df57143ff77c2778223277ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
