 {:db/id "q-110214" :question/id #uuid "49f435e1-94de-585c-a881-d3c848ebd176" :question/math-academy-id "q-110214" :question/problem "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=9).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first calculate the distribution of ${X}^{2}:$\n\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ | $81$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ | $0.2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we can immediately conclude that $P({X}^{2}=9)=0.4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-110214/ma-source-field/9ccb127ed7ea48dafdba822c5c339b8d5630506f6f0d8f4751bf5ed9a25d504d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
