 {:db/id "q-110382" :question/id #uuid "dcd0f3d5-ef5a-5674-9da2-afac7981af65" :question/math-academy-id "q-110382" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-110382/ma-source-field/efa32475dad141532738fdfa9d57f405733e37a5748e547c23b9cc1e96957f4d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
