 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "26c53a00-8010-5c1f-903e-01886e7b18f1"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 8 & 6 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-110384/ma-source-field/515ab47273771789fe8f76527c6af2e6921c7c86bac61ba6f4b39aadacaf9d69"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
