 {:db/id "q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6654880-b8cd-51ed-b3de-b163e5cfa06e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245/5b7ffd34cdce965bf58c59c9f5392f68ed244257fa5400d1fc2e9ad59ffcd71d" "q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245/0446fe32e53bff905dcf12854cc717acae086d5bcb545a9a74aabb32c9af8b5d" "q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245/099a53472eba76f4f0c836e92e737f6f748524ec895e6324026453ebaaf08593" "q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245/121e27e28b80c539bf505521fedc9abf0faba13e208f3b2133f8c61cded33382" "q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245/7fd1e3bad5ad169cfa4afbd74ed693bc5925e6033fb8072b56a6e865168aea4d"] :answer-field/correct "q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245/0446fe32e53bff905dcf12854cc717acae086d5bcb545a9a74aabb32c9af8b5d" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-110413" :question/id #uuid "d3c916b6-0455-5385-89d6-bc5b62f0897f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110413" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & -8 \\\\ -9 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =3.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-2:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nComputing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & -8 \\\\ -9 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & -8 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & -8 \\\\ -9 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 10 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(-2)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110413/ma-source-field/56cba56f79e5b3e6c7f50d8db12ae7a82dab6f3e50a8d3cad1feb3255e4ae245"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
