 {:db/id "q-110438" :question/id #uuid "7de0c22b-158b-598d-9b98-621628ea06b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110438" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block ${J}_{1}(-3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =-2:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block ${J}_{2}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely ${J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nComputing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ there will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110438/ma-source-field/6333f80a51317255b0d1b8563136d69b7c893e7ca8686aa5fc58238dd88bc464"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
