 {:db/id "q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab915e03-b049-5d6e-9faa-ad202c3447b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5/c0164cf136af52220771800c3701884e5b6dfff29b5e10934faf57a35ba45599" "q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5/b3a8f49e49d18503b36a451cb9bf7473a3333c1599d1b68d692cad11e322fb1b" "q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5/e388a7cca8d2e26834b368f9d50aee879d7d3f4a6a14a361cf2057cb452c513f" "q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5/6295676c521b85d957d0736e51f6a57fa4a5863983270a40aaa3eff00a8acdcc" "q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5/e6db707eb06488a7bf285519c80c9cf96bbe4a27d6818997394442b4e6a47e32"] :answer-field/correct "q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5/e6db707eb06488a7bf285519c80c9cf96bbe4a27d6818997394442b4e6a47e32" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-110499" :question/id #uuid "71312644-ebc0-5211-a7dd-54a8017a0c75" :question/math-academy-id "q-110499" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3\\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =1:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may have the single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block ${J}_{3}(1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may have a composition of one $2\\times 2$ and one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block, say ${J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we have in this situation, we find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$\n\nComputing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{3}(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110499/ma-source-field/96be223ad86a93a99a2327d39389b25fce744639a67f4a3e9da1c97054f0bab5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
