 {:db/id "q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5c176b-7979-5eff-a76f-39031b1756d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b/471e769ecd39727265d7199476614658b88b96efa318b2c7582d7b503da6b4de" "q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b/dc454497baa366e799de059fd4c0b94638a366bdfac76a6d1036112c96874973" "q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b/16331f32dc5a5aec902ed3f3994b57d6fecf99910fd1997afa477c27ed10b8f2" "q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b/1fb105916efba8f88d8c78d1c78da6ebb3f7d6ad4543fc53813bcce4da9764db" "q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b/93ca02f72917854b21153eef5f92f770f12a14007f6a6b0d26ba6b434c0577fc"] :answer-field/correct "q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b/16331f32dc5a5aec902ed3f3994b57d6fecf99910fd1997afa477c27ed10b8f2" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-110519" :question/id #uuid "e4031e9e-1517-5e53-b202-bd9db6b4d16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-110519" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3\\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =5:$\n\n- *Case I.* We may have the single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block ${J}_{3}(5).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may have a composition of one $2\\times 2$ and one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block, say ${J}_{2}(5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(5).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we have in this situation, we find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$\n\nComputing $A-5I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the 2nd  one). So, the rank of $A-5I$ is $1.$ Using the rank-nullity theorem, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-5I) & =3-\\text{rank}(A-5I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nRecall that the nullity of $A-5I$ gives the number of eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$ Therefore, there must be two such eigenvectors, say, ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}.$ \nSo, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(5)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110519/ma-source-field/83e7edbbdebefdf0f523382a749c17bd95e6c2b5a5162fe42dfdf8cc2906b86b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
